WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.133 --> 00:00:01.801
A group of Latino lawmakers

00:00:01.801 --> 00:00:03.036
in Connecticut are calling

00:00:03.036 --> 00:00:05.472
for a statewide ban of the term Latinx

00:00:06.139 --> 00:00:07.941
from official government documents.

00:00:07.941 --> 00:00:09.242
They say the gender neutral

00:00:09.242 --> 00:00:13.313
alternative to Latino or Latina is a term

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:14.881
that is offensive to Connecticut's

00:00:14.881 --> 00:00:16.783
large Puerto Rican population.

00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:17.283
Joining us

00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:18.551
now is more Democratic

00:00:18.551 --> 00:00:19.386
Connecticut state

00:00:19.386 --> 00:00:20.353
representative

00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:22.956
Heraldo Reyes, Jr, the bill's sponsor.

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:24.457
Thank you for your time.

00:00:24.457 --> 00:00:27.360
First, just explain to us, why do you

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.729
this term is offensive

00:00:31.464 --> 00:00:32.265
Well, thank you very much

00:00:32.265 --> 00:00:32.899
for the question.

00:00:32.899 --> 00:00:35.135
So I am one of the sponsors.

00:00:35.135 --> 00:00:36.536
I want to clarify that

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:38.671
there are six sponsors on the bill

00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:39.906
and Representative

00:00:39.906 --> 00:00:41.141
Rosario representing

00:00:41.141 --> 00:00:42.242
Gonzalez, representing

00:00:42.242 --> 00:00:44.044
Candelaria, Representative Sanchez

00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:46.413
and Representative Santiago and myself

00:00:46.413 --> 00:00:48.081
are all Puerto Rican

00:00:48.081 --> 00:00:49.849
Latino elected officials

00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:51.151
here in the state of Connecticut

00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:53.253
who all find the term offensive.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.990
And it's a term that we believe is

00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:59.726
unnecessary because the Spanish language,

00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:01.761
which is 50 plus years old,

00:01:02.662 --> 00:01:04.397
already identifies

00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:05.799
male, female and neutral

00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:07.867
and neutral.

00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:10.170
So we don't believe that it's necessary.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.872
If you don't like the word Latino

00:01:12.872 --> 00:01:13.473
or Latina,

00:01:13.473 --> 00:01:16.309
you can use Latin or or Latino,

00:01:16.309 --> 00:01:18.411
which is what the proper turn is for

00:01:18.745 --> 00:01:20.113
neutral gender.

00:01:20.113 --> 00:01:20.547
Hmm.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.582
Well, here's what the actor and comedian

00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:24.517
John Leguizamo says about it.

00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:25.718
He likes the term.

00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:27.754
Here's why he says, I love it.

00:01:28.021 --> 00:01:29.322
I think it's about time.

00:01:29.322 --> 00:01:31.357
I think it gets rid of the machismo

00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:33.226
that is not natural to our language,

00:01:33.226 --> 00:01:34.861
but got used by our language

00:01:34.861 --> 00:01:36.696
to promote male dominance.

00:01:36.696 --> 00:01:38.231
I love now that it's Latin.

00:01:38.231 --> 00:01:40.733
It's it's all inclusive, and I love that.

00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:41.968
So basically, he's saying that

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:45.338
that using Latino is gendered

00:01:46.473 --> 00:01:48.441
because it's the male

00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:50.510
oh, it's the male adjective.

00:01:53.313 --> 00:01:54.247
And that's correct.

00:01:54.247 --> 00:01:56.583
And and I respect John Leguizamo.

00:01:56.583 --> 00:01:58.218
I understand his

00:01:58.218 --> 00:01:59.586
his his brilliance,

00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:02.055
and I respect his work.

00:02:02.055 --> 00:02:03.957
But I will beg to differ him

00:02:03.957 --> 00:02:05.792
on that particular segment

00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:08.795
that whether it's in my term, in my

00:02:08.828 --> 00:02:11.097
by my thinking, it is offensive

00:02:11.598 --> 00:02:12.599
and I'll give an example.

00:02:12.599 --> 00:02:14.100
So I've had the opportunity

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.201
to travel

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:16.870
across these great United States

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:19.205
to speak with many Latino legislators

00:02:19.205 --> 00:02:21.541
and the majority of legislators

00:02:21.674 --> 00:02:22.275
in the state,

00:02:22.275 --> 00:02:23.710
in the United States of America

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:25.979
that are of Latino descent, are Mexican.

00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:28.148
And most of the Mexican

00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:29.716
American legislators

00:02:29.716 --> 00:02:31.618
find it very offensive

00:02:31.618 --> 00:02:32.886
and we've had this conversation

00:02:32.886 --> 00:02:33.653
with legislators

00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:34.587
from Texas,

00:02:34.587 --> 00:02:36.189
California, Arizona,

00:02:36.189 --> 00:02:37.524
Massachusetts, Rhode Island,

00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:39.092
New York,

00:02:39.092 --> 00:02:40.760
New Jersey, and obviously Connecticut.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:43.129
So my my my point being here

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:46.266
is that the majority, the high 96

00:02:46.266 --> 00:02:49.802
97% of the Latinos that speak Spanish

00:02:50.103 --> 00:02:51.371
find it offensive

00:02:51.371 --> 00:02:53.373
there is a small percentage of people

00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:54.307
that, again,

00:02:54.307 --> 00:02:56.609
this is not to be divisive in any way,

00:02:56.609 --> 00:02:58.678
and this is not to put off anybody.

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:00.079
This is simply a term

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:02.549
that we do not want to see in government

00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:03.917
and in higher education.

00:03:03.917 --> 00:03:05.485
It's not a ban of the word.

00:03:05.485 --> 00:03:06.619
It's a ban of the word

00:03:06.619 --> 00:03:07.153
and use

00:03:07.153 --> 00:03:09.289
it as it is related to government,

00:03:09.289 --> 00:03:10.957
official business

00:03:10.957 --> 00:03:13.660
So you talked about the percentage of

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:15.762
Latinos who, you know, who

00:03:15.795 --> 00:03:17.463
do not use this word

00:03:17.463 --> 00:03:18.831
or do not like the word.

00:03:18.831 --> 00:03:21.668
There's a Pew study that shows that most

00:03:21.668 --> 00:03:22.569
Latino adults

00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:24.704
have never even heard of the term Latin.

00:03:24.704 --> 00:03:27.106
76% have not heard of it.

00:03:27.106 --> 00:03:29.075
Another 20% do not use it.

00:03:29.075 --> 00:03:31.844
Only 3%, according to this Pew study,

00:03:32.445 --> 00:03:34.380
use the term Latinx.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.516
So you've got this legislation now.

00:03:36.516 --> 00:03:38.184
What's the possibility,

00:03:38.184 --> 00:03:39.819
the probability that it gets passed

00:03:41.788 --> 00:03:42.956
Well, thank you for the question.

00:03:42.956 --> 00:03:44.991
So there's there's a process

00:03:45.758 --> 00:03:46.292
we will have.

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:46.593
Public

00:03:46.593 --> 00:03:48.828
hearing is in general, administrative

00:03:50.463 --> 00:03:51.631
committee right now,

00:03:51.631 --> 00:03:53.433
and it's going through

00:03:53.433 --> 00:03:55.168
committee screening.

00:03:55.168 --> 00:03:56.669
What we what we are

00:03:56.669 --> 00:03:57.337
hoping to get

00:03:57.337 --> 00:03:58.905
is a public hearing very soon

00:03:58.905 --> 00:03:59.739
because we definitely want

00:03:59.739 --> 00:04:01.140
the input of the public.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:02.041
Number one.

00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:02.609
Number two,

00:04:02.609 --> 00:04:03.876
it comes out of committee

00:04:03.876 --> 00:04:05.678
and it goes to screening

00:04:05.678 --> 00:04:06.779
and it has to pass

00:04:06.779 --> 00:04:08.781
screening both in the majority

00:04:08.781 --> 00:04:11.851
and the speaker's screening process.

00:04:12.452 --> 00:04:13.253
Both sides,

00:04:13.253 --> 00:04:15.255
the majority in the minority party.

00:04:15.255 --> 00:04:16.923
Then when it passes that hurdle,

00:04:16.923 --> 00:04:19.292
then it goes to either it's a House bill.

00:04:19.559 --> 00:04:20.760
So it'll go to the House

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.362
of Representatives floor first.

00:04:22.362 --> 00:04:24.063
If it passes there, then it goes to that.

00:04:24.063 --> 00:04:25.131
The Senate,

00:04:25.131 --> 00:04:25.999
if it passes

00:04:25.999 --> 00:04:27.767
in, the governor can sign or veto it.

00:04:27.767 --> 00:04:28.668
So that's the process.

00:04:29.802 --> 00:04:30.370
All right,

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:31.938
Representative, for all the rest,

00:04:31.938 --> 00:04:33.673
thanks for explaining your perspective.

00:04:33.673 --> 00:04:35.041
Great to talk to you.

00:04:35.275 --> 00:04:36.376
Thank you very much for having me.

